## Context
PR #59 performance review identified that validate_page_range iterates items twice: once for membership check (c), once for ordering check (d). These can be merged into a single O(n) pass.

## Current State
File: crates/gossip-contracts/src/connector/page_validator.rs (lines 559-597)
Two separate for loops over items:
1. Loop (c): membership check — items.iter().enumerate() checking key in [start, end)
2. Loop (d): ordering check — items.iter().enumerate().skip(1) checking non-decreasing order

## Desired State
Single loop performing both membership and ordering checks per item. The key_bytes local from the membership check can be reused for the ordering comparison, eliminating one as_ref() call per item.

## Implementation Guidance
```rust
let mut prev: Option<&K> = None;
for (index, item) in items.iter().enumerate() {
    let key = item.item_key();
    let key_bytes = key.as_ref();
    // Membership: [start, end)
    let in_range = (start_unbounded || key_bytes >= start_bytes)
        && (end_unbounded || key_bytes < end_bytes);
    if !in_range { return Err(ItemKeyOutOfRange...); }
    // Ordering: non-decreasing
    if let Some(prev_key) = prev && prev_key.as_ref() > key_bytes {
        return Err(ItemsNotOrdered...);
    }
    prev = Some(key);
}
```

Note: error priority is preserved — membership checked before ordering for each item.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Single iteration loop for checks (c) and (d)
- [ ] All existing tests still pass
- [ ] Error priority preserved (ItemKeyOutOfRange reported before ItemsNotOrdered for same item)
- [ ] cargo test -p gossip-contracts --lib -- page_validator passes
- [ ] cargo clippy clean

## Context
The token consistency check in enumerate_page_bounds (lines 186-192) is provably a no-op. It gives the false impression of a safety mechanism while doing nothing.

## Current State
crates/gossip-connectors/src/in_memory.rs lines 180-193:
```rust
if let Some(last_key) = cursor.last_key() {
    let expected = upper_bound(&self.items, last_key.as_bytes());
    start_idx = start_idx.max(expected);  // After this: start_idx >= expected

    if self.emit_tokens
        && let Some(token) = cursor.token()
        && let Some(token_idx_u64) = parse_u64_be(token.as_bytes())
        && let Ok(token_idx) = usize::try_from(token_idx_u64)
        && token_idx == expected          // Guard: token_idx == expected
    {
        start_idx = start_idx.max(token_idx);  // NO-OP: start_idx >= expected, token_idx == expected
    }
}
```

Since start_idx is already >= expected (line 182) and the guard requires token_idx == expected, the max() on line 192 can never change start_idx.

## Desired State
Either delete the block entirely (simplest), or replace with a meaningful diagnostic:
```rust
#[cfg(debug_assertions)]
if self.emit_tokens {
    if let Some(token) = cursor.token() {
        if let Some(token_idx_u64) = parse_u64_be(token.as_bytes()) {
            if let Ok(token_idx) = usize::try_from(token_idx_u64) {
                debug_assert_eq!(token_idx, expected, "token/key resume position mismatch");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Dead code block removed or replaced with debug_assert
- [ ] cargo test passes
- [ ] cargo clippy passes
